To fix this code, the primary issue addressed was the improper handling of empty specification strings within ANSI escape sequences. The modified code now correctly throws an `IllegalArgumentException` when it encounters an empty specification, ensuring invalid inputs are detected and handled appropriately.

### Fixed Code
```java
    /**
     * Renders the given input to the target Appendable.
     *
     * @param input
     *            source to render
     * @param target
     *            render onto this target Appendable.
     * @return the given Appendable
     * @throws IOException
     *             If an I/O error occurs
     */
    public static Appendable render(final String input, Appendable target) throws IOException {

        int i = 0;
        int j, k;

        while (true) {
            j = input.indexOf(BEGIN_TOKEN, i);
            if (j == -1) {
                if (i == 0) {
                    target.append(input);
                    return target;
                }
                target.append(input.substring(i));
                return target;
            }
            target.append(input.substring(i, j));
            k = input.indexOf(END_TOKEN, j);

            if (k == -1) {
                target.append(input);
                return target;
            }
            j += BEGIN_TOKEN_LEN;
            String spec = input.substring(j, k);

            // Check for empty specification
            if (spec.isEmpty()) {
                throw new IllegalArgumentException("Empty ANSI escape specification");
            }

            String[] items = spec.split(CODE_TEXT_SEPARATOR, 2);
            if (items.length != 2) {
                throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid ANSI escape specification");
            }
            String replacement = render(items[1], items[0].split(CODE_LIST_SEPARATOR));

            target.append(replacement);

            i = k + END_TOKEN_LEN;
        }
    }
```

To fix this issue, we need to address scenarios where the specifier format is invalid or where tokens are improperly closed. Here's the corrected function:

```java
public static Appendable render(final String input, Appendable target) throws IOException {

    int i = 0;
    int j, k;

    while (true) {
        j = input.indexOf(BEGIN_TOKEN, i);
        if (j == -1) {
            if (i == 0) {
                target.append(input);
            } else {
                target.append(input.substring(i));
            }
            return target;
        }
        target.append(input.substring(i, j));
        k = input.indexOf(END_TOKEN, j);

        if (k == -1) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unclosed BEGIN token");
        }

        j += BEGIN_TOKEN_LEN;
        String spec = input.substring(j, k);

        String[] items = spec.split(CODE_TEXT_SEPARATOR, 2);
        if (items.length != 2) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid spec format expected two parts");
        }

        String replacement = render(items[1], items[0].split(CODE_LIST_SEPARATOR));

        target.append(replacement);

        i = k + END_TOKEN_LEN;
    }
}
